></p:txBody></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="171" name="Google Shape;171;p18"/><p:cNvSpPr txBox="1"/><p:nvPr><p:ph idx="1" type="body"/></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="819150" y="1990725"/><a:ext cx="7505700" cy="2448000"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom></p:spPr><p:txBody><a:bodyPr anchorCtr="0" anchor="t" bIns="91425" lIns="91425" spcFirstLastPara="1" rIns="91425" wrap="square" tIns="91425"><a:noAutofit/></a:bodyPr><a:lstStyle/><a:p><a:pPr indent="457200" lvl="0" marL="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t>Bose-</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t>Einstein</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t> - Bose-Einstein is a very </a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t>weird</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t> state of matter. It is a state of matter </a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t>in which</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t> molecules are cooled to Absolute Zero. Absolute Zero is impossible to reach but scientists try to get as close as they possibly can. When atoms are this cold they begin to get so tight and jammed together. They cant move and they begin to stop. They become to close that they turn into “1 single molecule”. This state of matter is still being studied as there still </a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t>isn't</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t> too much known about it. Even though Bose-Einstein is a state of matter nearly nothing is a Bose-Einstein because of the fact that nothing can be absolute zero.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="457200" lvl="0" marL="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="1600"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="1600"/></a:spcAft><a:buNone/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t>	Now depending the temperature the faster or slower molecules move. The hotter the temperature the faster the </a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t>molecules</a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr lang="en"/><a:t> move and the colder it is the slower they move. </a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr id="172" name="Google Shape;172;p18"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:stretch><a:fillRect/></a:stretch></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="217025" y="3943925"/><a:ext cx="993200" cy="988225"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr><mc:AlternateContent><mc:Choice Requires="p14"><p:transition spd="slow" p14:dur="4500"><p14:flip dir="l"/></p:transition></mc:Choice><mc:Fallback><p:transition spd="slow"><p:fade/></p:transition></mc:Fallback></mc:AlternateContent></p:sld>